From d9fe2f1762f16b85f94c1c6e69a817f2efbfcb1c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <1315-Harvester57@users.noreply.git.frama-c.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 01:41:43 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [meta] do not copy callee Old/Pre/Here labels into \written
 and \read instances

When a \writing or \reading property is checked at a call to a function
whose footprint comes from its contract (an undefined callee, under the
default -meta-check-ext, or -meta-check-callee-assigns), inspect_contract
substitutes the formals of each assigns location (or \from location, for
\read) and of the assumes clauses by the actual arguments, and the result
becomes \written or \read in an assertion attached to the call.

The terms and assumes predicates are copied with the labels of the
contract. In an assigns clause, a labelled logic function called without
a label (strlen(s) in the libc specification of strcpy, for instance) is
typed with Old, and a user may write \old(*p), \at(*p, Pre) or
\at(*p, Here). In an assumes clause, \valid(p), labelled logic functions
such as len(p), and \at(*p, Here) are typed with Here, and \at(*p, Pre)
with Pre. In the generated assertion:

- Old is not a valid label in a statement annotation. -check reports
  "Logic label Old is not declared in this scope", the kernel rejects the
  printed code ("logic label `Old' not found"), WP stops with
  Wp__NormAtLabels.LabelError and E-ACSL with "invalid label 'Old' within
  statement annotation 'Assertion'".
- Pre is valid but denotes the pre-state of the caller, not the state
  before the call, so the instance silently checks another location.
- Here is valid, and matches the pre-state when the assertion precedes
  the call, but when a property uses After the assertion is placed after
  the call, so Here silently evaluates in the post-state instead of the
  callee's pre-state.

All three labels denote the pre-state of the callee, which is the state
just before the call. Map them to the meta label Before in the
substitution visitor: replace_after_before already turns it into Here
when the assertion precedes the call, and into a C label on the call
(added by instantiate) when it is placed after it.

New test tests/meta/callee_labels.c covers an implicit Old, \old,
\at(., Pre), \at(., Here) and an After property for \written, Old and Pre
in \from parts for \read, and assumes clauses (\valid, labelled logic
function, Here and Pre) under both before-call and After properties, in
the default and fold configurations.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
